ndard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What are the connected subsets of $\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["Only singleton sets","Intervals","Only open sets","Only rational subsets"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0008d79d976b98cc71107c87","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_connected_and_disconnected_sets_52bfd2a8_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $\\{V,W\\}$ is a disjoint open cover of $E$, but $E\\cap W = \\varnothing$. Does this show that $E$ is disconnected?","options":["Yes, because the cover is open","Yes, because $V$ and $W$ are disjoint","No, because open covers are irrelevant to disconnectedness","No, because both $E\\cap V$ and $E\\cap W$ must be nonempty"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0e8cec37ef56f568b4bebd2e","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_connected_and_disconnected_sets_52bfd2a8_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can disconnectedness not be tested before specifying a topology or another structure that defines open sets?","options":["Disconnectedness only applies to ordered sets.","The definition uses open sets to separate the space.","Disconnectedness requires a probability measure.","The definition uses differentiability at every point."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"954cf4dfd421e4b2209049a4","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_connected_and_disconnected_sets_52bfd2a8_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Under this definition, why should one be careful before calling the empty set connected?","options":["The empty set is always an interval under this definition","The empty set has exactly two points","The empty set is a disconnected example","Connected means nonempty and not disconnected"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ab302e047e695055dfb5e637","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_connected_and_disconnected_sets_52bfd2a8_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f\\colon X\\to Y$ be continuous and let $E\\subseteq X$ be connected. What can be concluded about $f(E)$?","options":["$f(E)$ is connected in $Y$","$f(E)$ is disconnected in $Y$","$f(E)$ must be a singleton","$f(E)$ must equal $Y$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c726422f195843b5d1ad858","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_connected_and_disconnected_sets_52bfd2a8_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $E = \\{a,b\\}$ with $a \\neq b$ in a discrete metric space. Which statement follows?","options":["$E$ is disconnected because singleton sets are open in a discrete metric space","$E$ is connected because it has only two points","$E$ is connected because it is finite","$E$ is disconnected only if $a$ and $b$ are rational numbers, since then a gap separates them"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"192c7eda623c7a6acedf7ae4","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_connected_and_disconnected_sets_52bfd2a8_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What must be true of $E\\cap V$ and $E\\cap W$ in a disconnection by $\\{V,W\\}$?","options":["Both must be empty.","Both must be nonempty.","Only $E\\cap V$ may be nonempty.","They must be equal to $X$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5e91dc34133de4e7fa340e49","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_connected_and_disconnected_sets_52bfd2a8_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which set is disconnected as a subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ by having two separated pieces?","options":["$(0,3)$","$[0,1]$","$(0,1)\\cup(2,3)$","$\\{0\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a1c3d792b361b89438d83c53","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_connected_and_disconnected_sets_52bfd2a8_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose open sets $V,W$ cover $E$ and $E\\cap V\\cap W=\\varnothing$, but $E\\cap W=\\varnothing$. Why does this fail to prove that $E$ is disconnected?","options":["Disconnectedness requires the cover to be finite but not open.","Disconnectedness ignores intersections with $E$.","Disconnectedness requires $V=W$.","Disconnectedness requires both pieces of $E$ to be nonempty."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1ae9fd9ea4cf6fc0d792f345","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"repair_connected_and_disconnected_sets_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the intuitive meaning of connectedness?","options":["The set comes in one piece without a split into two nonempty separated open pieces.","The set has exactly two points.","The set is always finite.","The set is a subset of $\\mathbb{Q}$, so no irrational point can ever split it into two pieces."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"51c0563d5